The best approach to use the Claude API without memory expenses is to achieve
Stateless Efficiency by keeping the "context window" as small and static as 
possible.Here is the ultimate workflow setup to minimize costs:1. Leverage Prompt 
Caching (The 90% Discount)Claude offers a feature called Prompt Caching [1]. If 
your system prompt, tools, or massive reference code blocks stay exactly the same 
between messages, Claude caches them [1].How it saves money: You get up to a 90% 
discount on reading those tokens again [1].The Catch: The cache only lasts for 5 
minutes of inactivity [1].Action: Group your work into focused, fast-paced bursts
of 5 minutes so you constantly hit the active cache instead of paying full price.
2. Implement the "Summarize & Restart" StrategyInstead of letting a chat thread g
row long and expensive, manually manage its memory:Work in a thread until it reac
hes about 10–15 messages.Send this prompt: "Summarize our progress, the current 
state of the code, and the next immediate steps in a concise bulleted list."Copy
that summary, close the chat, open a fresh thread, and paste the summary as your
new starting point. This completely wipes away the expensive middle history.3. 
Use "System Prompts" for Static ContextIf you must provide a lot of background in
formation (like documentation or a project overview), put it inside the System 
Prompt instead of the user message.Many API UIs (like LibreChat, OpenWebUI, or 
SillyTavern) have a specific box for "System Prompt."Claude targets the system 
prompt first for caching, making it much cheaper to keep around.4. Route Easy 
Tasks to Claude 3.5 HaikuNever use Claude 3 Opus or 3.5 Sonnet for basic tasks.
Route tasks like text formatting, simple debugging, regex generation, or data 
parsing to Claude 3.5 Haiku.Haiku is exceptionally fast and costs a mere fraction
of Sonnet's price, meaning a massive chat history there will only cost cents instead
of euros.5. Strip Your Code and FilesNever upload an entire folder or a 2,000-line 
file if you only need help with a 50-line function.Delete comments, logs, and 
unrelated functions before pasting code.If using coding agents (like Cline or
Roo Code), change their settings from "Auto-read workspace" to "Manual file 
selection" so they don't secretly upload your whole hard drive on every turn.
